<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Evagoras I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 374 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Evagoras I was king of Salamis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from 411 to 374 BCE. He promoted Greek culture, extended his rule over much of the island and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. After a war of about ten years he was besieged in Salamis, but in 380 BCE he obtained a peace that left him king as a Persian tributary. He was murdered in 374 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a> wrote an encomium in his praise.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/evagoras-i.jpg" alt="Evagoras I" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerhard.Haubold · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evagoras-Stater_733597_C.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
